I judged this function **not vulnerable** and wrote the answer to `results/s126.json`.

The two places where this kind of code usually goes wrong are both guarded:
- **Number of entries:** the loop stops once `num_ranges` reaches `VBIOS_MAX_AC_TIMING_ENTRIES` (lines 13–14). So writing to `table->mc_reg_table_entry[num_ranges]` can't go past the end of the array.
- **Copying the previous value:** the `DATA_EQU_PREV` branch checks `if (i)` (line 30) before reading `mc_data[i-1]`. That stops an out-of-bounds read at index -1 when the first register is marked "same as previous".

From memory, both guards look like later fixes added to this kernel code, so this sample is probably the patched version.

Two things can't be checked from this file alone. Both depend on how other code sets things up:
- whether `table->last` is always within the size of the `mc_data` array;
- whether reads through `reg_data` stay inside the VBIOS table.

The suggested-fix field of the JSON lists these as optional hardening, not as vulnerabilities.
